  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT SAT OCT 17 2026\n\nThis evening a large ridge stretches across much of the CONUS \nfrom New England to the Great Lakes to Texas and the Deep South. A\n1035 mb center is now over Ohio. Surface pressure gradients are \nbeginning to relax as ridging settles across the region. Winds are\nresponding to this change from yesterday and beginning to taper \ndown tonight. Winds should start to become NE to E on Sunday and \nnot quite as gusty in response to placement adjustments of the \nsurface ridge. Cool fall readings are on tap with lows in the 40s \nfor most of this week. We could even see an isolated low or two in\nthe upper 30s in a few of the colder northern locales and valleys\nfor Sunday and Monday nights. Highs will be generally in the 60s \nfor Sunday with a few lower 70s. While overall Eastern Conus \nridging continues this week, it does weaken a little. As such, our\nairmass should begin to moderate as we head through the upcoming \nweek. Look for highs by Monday to be seasonal in the 70s, with \nlower 80s creeping back for some by Wednesday. However, we will \nremain rainfree with very little moisture available for cloud \ncover, much less rain.\n\n08",
